   adoption of far memory technologies in data centers to provide
   cost-effective, high-capacity memory solutions. However, far memory
   presents new performance challenges because its access latencies are
   significantly longer and more variable than local DRAM. For applications
   to achieve acceptable performance on far memory, a high degree of
   memory-level parallelism (MLP) is needed to tolerate the long access
   latency. While modern out-of-order processors are capable of exploiting
   a certain degree of MLP, they are constrained by resource limitations
   and hardware complexity. The key obstacle is the synchronous memory},
